[
  {
    "lesson_id": "064",
    "trigger_word": "retreat",
    "trigger_card_number": 608,
    "title": "LECCIÓN 64: HAVING SAID THAT",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "La lección 54 te enseñó a LEER las",
          "cláusulas de participio. Ahora las",
          "escribes tú.",
          "",
          "Dos acciones, un sujeto, una frase:",
          "\"They lost the harbour. They retreated.\"",
          "pasa a ser",
          "\"Having lost the harbour, they",
          "retreated.\"",
          "",
          "-ing = a la vez, o justo antes."
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "La regla que importa: el participio",
          "pertenece al sujeto PRINCIPAL.",
          "",
          "Bien: \"Having lost the harbour, the",
          "army retreated.\"",
          "Mal: \"Having lost the harbour, the",
          "retreat began.\" — una retirada no puede",
          "perder un puerto.",
          "",
          "Forma pasiva: -ed, o being + -ed.",
          "\"Built in 1890, the mill still stands.\"",
          "",
          "Fórmulas fijas: having said that,",
          "given that, assuming that.",
          "",
          "El español enlaza con una conjunción y",
          "repite el sujeto; el inglés suprime las",
          "dos cosas y se apoya en el participio."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "065",
    "trigger_word": "luggage",
    "trigger_card_number": 662,
    "title": "LECCIÓN 65: THE MAN ARRESTED",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Una oración de relativo puede perder su",
          "who o which Y su be, sin perder nada",
          "más.",
          "",
          "\"The luggage WHICH WAS left on the",
          "platform\" pasa a",
          "\"The luggage left on the platform\".",
          "",
          "\"The man WHO WAS arrested\" pasa a",
          "\"The man arrested\"."
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Así el inglés apila descripción en",
          "torno a un solo sustantivo:",
          "\"the luggage left on the platform at",
          "Crewe on Tuesday night\"",
          "",
          "Las capas, en orden:",
          "[determinante] [adjetivo] SUSTANTIVO",
          "[frase preposicional] [participio]",
          "",
          "Solo caen who/which + be. No se puede",
          "reducir \"the man who owns the shop\":",
          "ahí no hay be que quitar.",
          "",
          "El español pone la descripción antes o",
          "conserva el relativo; el inglés cuelga",
          "una cola larga detrás del sustantivo."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "066",
    "trigger_word": "commitment",
    "trigger_card_number": 708,
    "title": "LECCIÓN 66: THE INCREASE IN FEES",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "El inglés académico convierte verbos y",
          "adjetivos en sustantivos, y construye",
          "sobre ellos.",
          "",
          "commit -> commitment",
          "increase -> the increase",
          "decide -> the decision",
          "able -> ability",
          "",
          "\"The team committed\" pasa a",
          "\"The commitment of the team\"."
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "¿Para qué? El sustantivo admite",
          "modificadores, y así una frase carga",
          "más de una vez:",
          "",
          "\"The commitment of the whole team",
          "explains the change in their results.\"",
          "",
          "Terminaciones frecuentes: -ment, -tion,",
          "-sion, -ance, -ence, -ity, -ness, -al.",
          "",
          "Úsalo con tiento. Demasiada",
          "nominalización espesa la prosa y",
          "esconde quién hizo qué.",
          "",
          "El español también nominaliza mucho. Lo",
          "propio del inglés es la cadena de of",
          "que viene detrás."
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
]
